#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100642 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbb4e0a0-55ec-5cc7-9617-99cec8108940" :question/math-academy-id "e-540" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =5x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm {e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & =\\pm {e}^{C}{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3 \\\\ K{e}^{5\\cdot 0} & =3 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x}  \\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891e9004-961b-539a-ab93-0752347fd077" :question/math-academy-id "e-541" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that  $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0.$ Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} .$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C.$\n\nNow let's apply the condition $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$ to find $C_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =0+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y)   =  \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4641fa43-5ee8-5a98-a998-8306f96eab45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290369} {:answer/id #uuid "d67abf21-4258-5d25-a6bf-4116fb2dc38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290370} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc01640-f9b4-5f31-9081-7b2cadc0f221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290371} {:answer/id #uuid "d3937128-3f8b-53e0-91dc-8c5fd9f9b870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290372} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb4d639-0119-517a-9dff-02201f38f90f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d67abf21-4258-5d25-a6bf-4116fb2dc38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290370} :answer-field/id #uuid "a51a5481-c1f2-538c-bf44-ea05615e2d8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eab8a97a-f952-5f16-9442-1a3ecf58a82a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50220" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{1+C} \\\\ 1+C & =1 \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}=0$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"}]}
